\begin_layout Abstract
This study explores various machine learning techniques for assessing forest fire risk.
 The data used in this study have been selected from three different areas in Greece over the past 25 years (2000 - 2024).
 The first area of interest is Attica,
 the second area is eastern Macedonia and Thrace,
 and the third area is Crete.
 For each of the aforementioned areas,
 a separate data file was created,
 an analysis was carried out,
 and specialized definitions - classes were created for the forest fire risk occurrence.
 This study utilized a range of traditional machine learning techniques alongside novel methods based on Grammatical Evolution,
 including automated feature construction from existing attributes and the automatic generation of classification rules.
 For the Attica dataset,
 our results showed that GENCLASS achieved an error rate of 12.43%,
 corresponding to an accuracy of 87.57%.
 For the Eastern Macedonia & Thrace dataset,
 FC2 (MLP) reached an error rate of 11.87% and an accuracy of 88.13%,
 while GENCLASS performed very similarly with 11.93% error and 88.07% accuracy.
 Finally,
 for the Crete dataset,
 GENCLASS recorded an error rate of 15.11%,
 resulting in an accuracy of 84.89%.
 GENCLASS demonstrated stable performance across all three datasets,
 validating the climatological attributes and the definitions that underpin the construction of the Final_DangerClass_combination.
 
\end_layout

