 wind speed,
 wind direction,
 time of occurrence,
 and month.
 Continuing,
 the research by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Yunhao 2007"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 compared two wildfire models,
 the IFSI and the FPI,
 reporting accuracy levels of 78% and 73%,
 respectively.
 The difference compared to our work lies both in our superior results and in the application of our own customized rules.The study by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Sakr 2011"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 took a different direction,
 focusing on only two features:
 humidity and precipitation,
 and achieved high accuracy levels of 97%.
 The main difference compared to our work lies in the number of features used,
 as we focused on eight features,
 with our accuracy averaging 86.84%.
 Although the following aim 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Agarwal 2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 combined operational wildfire data with meteorological data,
 it focused on estimating the economic damage caused by fires,
 achieving very strong results ranging from 93.3% to 97%.
 While the core structure of the data,
 namely the integration of operational and meteorological information,
 aligns with our own approach,
 the fundamental discrepancy lies in the fact that their work targeted economic loss estimation,
 whereas our research focused on deriving insights that would be operationally useful for wildfire suppression services.
 The subsequent researchers 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Masinda 2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 attempted to verify the effectiveness of the Canadian FWI model in a region of China,
 reporting an accuracy of 72.7%.
 The distinction from our study lies in the fact that we developed our own 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 tailored to the meteorological characteristics of each region,
 with better results.
 Using the same model,
 the next project from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Karali 2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 on wildfire prediction for the region of Attica,
 where their results reached 60%.
 This comparison is particularly interesting,
 as they worked on one of the regions we also analyzed.
 Our results for Attica reached 87.57% accuracy and 12.43% error.
 Since as previously noted we did not rely on external models or generic rules,
 but instead developed our own region specific rules.The final study 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Parvar 2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 combined meteorological data with operational fire data,
 focusing specifically on land surface temperature and precipitation,
 achieving high performance with a ROC value reaching 0.83.
 Although the core idea and the data integration approach are similar to ours,
 we employed a larger set of meteorological variables,
 achieving correspondingly high accuracy levels.
 
